{
 "id": "2018-garridovelarde-building",
 "name": "Building and Background Trees - Height Ratio Integration Rating",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the original photographs: 18 photographs of isolated rural houses on or near the skyline, three in Norrland, northern Sweden, and three in the Ambroz Valley, Cáceres, Spain, each digitally edited into three versions that change only the height of the trees behind the building: A, vegetation does not exceed the eaves; B, vegetation exceeds 0% to 50% of the building's height; C, vegetation exceeds 100% of the building's height. Participants give gender, age, country of origin and educational background, the paper's four background topics, then see the 18 photographs one at a time in random order, as in the paper, and answer the paper's question How would you rate the integration of the building in this scene? on its 5-point scale from 1 = very bad to 5 = very good. Rebuilt from Garrido-Velarde, Montero-Parejo, Hernández-Blanco & García-Moruno (2018), Visual Analysis of the Height Ratio between Building and Background Vegetation. Two Rural Cases of Study: Spain and Sweden, Sustainability 10(8): 2593, with the photographs cropped from its Figure 3 and the question from Section 2.4 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: s1A-s6C = scenario 1-6 and height-ratio level A-C of Figure 3 (scenarios 1-3 Swedish, 4-6 Spanish); answers are in integration, keyed by image id; each image's scenario, country and height-ratio level are stored in the page's original-study block. Analysis as in the paper: mean integration rating per photograph and per height-ratio level, and a 2 (respondent country) x 3 (height ratio, within subjects) ANOVA with Bonferroni pairwise tests and Cohen's d; the paper (76 university students, 44 in Spain and 32 in Sweden) found that in both countries level B, vegetation up to half the building's height above it, was rated best and integration was good or very good up to that level, while level C was rated lowest by the Swedish students and mid-scale by the Spanish students, and Swedish students gave higher ratings than Spanish students at levels A and B. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper showed the photographs in face-to-face interviews with a researcher present, in Spanish and English; here the survey is online, in English, and anyone can take part; (2) the wording of the background questions and the instruction text are ours, because the paper lists only their topics; (3) the photographs are the figure copies (about 950-1040 px wide).",
